"""Original introductory case-management reasoning, not a practitioner licence."""
def course(c,m):
 return c("SWP-051","GBV Case Management and Safety Planning","gbv-case-management-and-safety-planning","GBV Case Management\nand Safety Planning",
 "Prepare a fictional case-support framework with specific consent, survivor-defined priorities, qualified safety-planning support, bounded information sharing and supervised review.",[
m("Casework roles consent and service readiness","Explain the conditions required for specialist case management and distinguish specific consent from blanket permission.",[
"GBV case management is a structured professional process supporting a survivor's priorities and access to services over time. It involves more than making a referral or keeping a form. Qualified staff need suitable training, supervision, organisational procedures, secure information systems and functioning referral relationships. The inter-agency guidelines and programme standards are benchmarks for those arrangements; completing this introductory course does not establish specialist competence or authorise independent practice. In the fictional exercise, identify the responsible professional role and the service conditions that would be needed before a casework plan could be delivered. [S11, S10]",
"Consent is an ongoing informed discussion about a particular action, its purpose, relevant risks and available choices. Separate agreement to receive a service from agreement to create a record, share information or contact another organisation. A signature does not provide unlimited future permission and should not be treated as proof that the person understood or freely chose every subsequent step. Explain relevant confidentiality limits under verified local law and procedures before gathering sensitive information. The course does not decide Nigerian mandatory-reporting duties or permit a worker to invent an exception because another agency requests information. [S11, S1]",
"Clarify what the service can actually offer and how an individual can decline a particular action without being blamed. A person may want information or immediate assistance while remaining uncomfortable with documentation or a proposed referral. The guidelines support responding to concerns and preserving appropriate help rather than treating organisational paperwork as the purpose of support. Ask what options are feasible under the applicable procedures and professional role. Provider-perspective research identifies concerns about service control and access, but does not show that a template or a worker's preferred plan guarantees safety, trust or recovery. [S11, R22, S1]",
"Check readiness before assigning responsibility in a plan. Verify the professional role, supervisory support, private setting, accessible communication, current referral capacity and proportionate record arrangements. Record an unconfirmed condition as pending rather than describe another service's name as an accepted commitment. The Nigerian help-seeking study can prompt questions about different support pathways, while its 2018 survey classes neither certify current services nor determine an individual's preferred source of help. If the required specialist conditions are absent, keep the learner within basic support and an appropriate referral route instead of improvising case management. [R21, S10, S11]"],
"Fictional case: a service wants a newly trained volunteer to manage a case alone using a downloaded form. The volunteer identifies missing supervision, information safeguards and referral capacity. In the exercise, consent to an initial discussion is kept separate from recording and information sharing.",
"Prepare a readiness-and-consent note with responsible qualified role, supervisor, private/access arrangements, referral capacity and three distinct permission decisions. Identify an unmet condition that prevents independent casework readiness.",
"A form and an MCQ pass do not establish professional readiness. Consent must remain specific and informed, while qualified roles, supervision, capacity and secure procedures need actual confirmation.",["S11","S10","S1","R22","R21"],[
("Case management requires organisational and professional conditions.","A volunteer has passed an introductory MCQ course but has no supervision or secure records. What is accurate?","Specialist casework readiness is not established.",["The MCQ pass alone authorises independent case management.","A downloaded form supplies all missing safeguards.","A service name guarantees specialist capacity."],"Professional competence and systems cannot be inferred from short-course completion."),
("Consent remains specific throughout a case.","A person agrees to receive support but has not agreed to information sharing. What should the case framework show?","A separate informed decision is needed for the proposed sharing, subject to verified lawful limits.",["A blanket permission for every later use.","Automatic permission to contact all relatives.","A rule that a signature removes the need for explanation."],"An agreement to one action is not all-encompassing consent."),
("Confidentiality limits need verified local grounding.","A worker says all GBV disclosures must always be reported under an invented universal rule. What should be corrected?","Use the actual applicable legal and safeguarding process with appropriate qualified advice and clear explanation.",["Keep the invented rule because it simplifies paperwork.","Treat another agency's curiosity as a lawful exception.","Promise absolute confidentiality regardless of established duties."],"Neither invented reporting duties nor unrestricted secrecy is appropriate."),
("Documentation concerns should not be dismissed.","An adult wants appropriate help but is concerned about a case file. What should a qualified service discuss?","The concern, relevant procedures and feasible support choices without treating paperwork as the goal.",["A threat to withdraw all respectful assistance immediately.","Secret creation of a personal worker's file.","A claim that records carry no possible privacy concern."],"The guidelines centre assistance and informed choices rather than form completion."),
("Referral capacity requires current confirmation.","A case plan names an organisation that has not accepted a role. What status is accurate?","A proposed pathway awaiting confirmation.",["A confirmed service commitment.","Completed support because a name was entered.","A guaranteed result for the survivor."],"Naming an organisation does not establish its agreement or capacity."),
]),
m("Survivor-defined priorities and safety planning","Describe a supported planning process that avoids universal prescriptions and unsupported safety guarantees.",[
"A case-support discussion begins with what the person identifies as most important, not the worker's preferred relationship outcome. Needs may concern immediate health, safety, communication, housing, care, livelihood or another priority. Ask only necessary questions through a qualified, consent-based assessment and avoid repeated retelling across services. Do not assume that leaving, returning, reporting or involving family is always the safest option. The inter-agency guidelines place the survivor's wishes and circumstances at the centre, while this introductory exercise practises reasoning about roles and conditions rather than delivering an individual professional assessment. [S11, S1]",
"Safety planning is an individual, changing process undertaken with appropriately trained support. It considers what the person sees as risks, feasible options, trusted support, safe communication and conditions requiring prompt qualified attention. A generic checklist or a completed worksheet cannot guarantee safety. An item that is helpful for one person, such as carrying a printed plan or receiving a message, may expose another person's service contact. The fictional task should identify these questions and relevant professional responsibilities, without writing a universal escape instruction or directing contact with a person who may cause harm. [S11, S10]",
"Maintain a distinction between social support and clinical, legal or forensic decisions. A potential urgent concern should connect promptly to the relevant authorised arrangements, with the person's involvement and consent wherever possible; routine planning should not delay appropriate care. A learner should not diagnose trauma, prescribe medication, collect forensic evidence or determine legal entitlements. Situations involving children, adolescents, supported decision-making or safeguarding need the appropriate specialist process and current local advice. The adult fictional exercise cannot establish that an accompanying person is a safe decision-maker or resolve an exception to confidentiality. [S11, S1]",
"Turn a chosen priority into a bounded plan with an agreed action, qualified owner, confirmed or pending service role, communication condition and review point. State what would need reassessment if circumstances or preferences change. The provider-perspective synthesis highlights choice and system accessibility but does not supply a tested Nigerian safety-planning instrument. The Nigerian help-seeking study also cannot validate a particular trusted contact. Treat each proposed support as something to discuss and verify rather than an assumption. A person can revise an earlier choice; the plan should preserve a feasible route for that discussion without blaming them for changing priorities. [R22, R21, S11]"],
"Fictional case: an adult prioritises an accessible health enquiry and safe communication, while a worker assumes that a printed relocation plan is needed. The learner identifies the assumption, the risks of revealing materials and the qualified roles needed to discuss options without imposing an outcome.",
"Create a fictional support-plan outline with chosen priority, qualified action owner, service status, communication condition, review point and two assumptions to check. Explain why it cannot certify safety or replace an individual specialist assessment.",
"The outline should preserve the person's priority and changing choices. It identifies professional boundaries and context-specific communication risks instead of prescribing departure, family contact or a universally safe checklist.",["S11","S1","S10","R22","R21"],[
("A plan begins with the person's priorities.","A worker assumes every adult's goal is to leave immediately. What should a survivor-centred assessment do?","Explore the person's own priorities and circumstances through the appropriate qualified process.",["Apply the same relationship decision to everyone.","Condition help on agreeing with the worker.","Treat a different preference as proof of dishonesty."],"A professional plan should not substitute the worker's preferred outcome."),
("Safety planning is individual and revisable.","A completed generic worksheet is described as a guarantee of safety. What is accurate?","A worksheet cannot guarantee safety; individual assessment and continuing review remain necessary.",["Every checked box removes all risk.","The same plan is safe in every context.","No changed circumstance needs review."],"Safety concerns and feasible options can change."),
("A visible plan can create an information risk.","An adult says a printed support plan could be found by someone controlling them. What needs consideration?","Whether the format and communication arrangement are safe in that person's circumstances.",["A rule that every survivor must take a printed plan.","An assumption that written materials are harmless.","Permission to send the plan to shared contacts without checking."],"The medium itself can reveal a sensitive service relationship."),
("A short course does not confer forensic or clinical authority.","A learner is asked to collect forensic evidence and prescribe care during a fictional case discussion. What should be identified?","Those decisions require relevant qualified professional roles and procedures.",["The course title as sufficient authority.","A worksheet as a clinical protocol.","A general research citation as permission to prescribe."],"The course teaches bounded support reasoning, not specialist clinical or forensic practice."),
("Changing preferences should prompt review rather than blame.","A person revises a previously chosen support option. What should the plan allow?","A feasible discussion of the new preference and circumstances through the agreed process.",["A penalty for changing their mind.","Permanent enforcement of the earlier choice.","Removal of all opportunities for feedback."],"Supported planning is an ongoing process rather than a fixed contract for behaviour."),
]),
m("Follow-up supervision and restricted information","Plan accountable follow-up and case review within qualified systems and specific permission.",[
"Follow-up checks whether the agreed support was reachable, remains useful and still matches the person's priorities, using the communication arrangements they have accepted. A failed referral, changed circumstance or new concern may require a revised plan and appropriate qualified attention. Do not equate attendance, a referral sent or a closed file with a demonstrated safety or recovery outcome. The inter-agency standards support accountable service quality, while the actual interpretation needs appropriate evidence and the person's perspective. In this course, record only an invented case sequence; no learner should create a real survivor record to show that follow-up has been practised. [S11, S10, S1]",
"Supervision should support competent decisions and worker accountability without turning a personal account into general circulation. Use the authorised secure system, relevant access restrictions and minimum necessary information for the supervisory purpose. Consent, information-sharing arrangements and applicable lawful limits remain important; a request from a supervisor does not justify posting identifiable details in a group chat. A general consultation can often be framed without identifiers, while a specific professional need must follow the appropriate process. Do not store case data in a personal AI service, unapproved cloud drive or coursework submission. [S11, S1]",
"Review the service's contribution honestly. Provider accounts can help identify practical pressures such as fragmented services and limited resources, but they should not replace appropriately gathered survivor feedback or be treated as a causal evaluation of recovery. The qualitative synthesis draws on high-income Western settings and reports inconsistent included-study counts, which are retained in the evidence note. Use its concerns to frame local questions about continuity, choice and barriers, not to claim the same outcomes everywhere. Record the action owner and unresolved dependency when a service cannot deliver what the plan anticipated. [R22, S10]",
"A case closure or transfer needs the relevant professional process, a discussion of the person's circumstances and preferences, and clarity about any continuing support or later return route. Do not close a file simply to improve performance counts or pursue unwanted contact to complete a target. Explain known service limits and preserve the approved record history and feedback route. Where a concern or complaint challenges a decision, use the appropriate review procedure without retaliating or sharing identifying details unnecessarily. These are conditions for supervised practice; an offline course score and a fictional plan do not authorise a school certificate or a specialist caseworker designation. [S11, S1, S10]"],
"Fictional case: a referral did not reach the intended service. At an authorised supervisory review, the team examines the gap using necessary information, revisits agreed contact arrangements and records a qualified owner's response. It does not close the case to meet a target or post details to a general chat.",
"Write a fictional follow-up and supervision note with the failed step, permission/contact boundary, necessary information, qualified review role, response owner and a condition for later review or appropriate closure.",
"The note should distinguish outputs from access and retain a route to respond. Qualified supervision, restricted information and the person's priorities matter more than contact or closure targets.",["S11","S10","S1","R22"],[
("Follow-up asks about useful access rather than paperwork alone.","A plan records a referral but the receiving service never responded. What is a relevant review question?","Whether the intended support was reachable and what appropriate next step the person wants.",["Whether sending the referral proves recovery.","Whether the file can be counted as successful without enquiry.","Whether the person should be blamed for the missing response."],"The useful pathway extends beyond the outgoing referral."),
("Supervision does not justify unrestricted disclosure.","A general group chat asks for a survivor's identifying case details. What should guide the worker?","Use the authorised necessary-information process and avoid broad disclosure.",["Assume any supervisory label removes confidentiality duties.","Send the full file because a colleague asked.","Upload the record to a personal AI account for convenience."],"Access and purpose restrictions continue during professional consultation."),
("Provider perspectives do not replace survivor outcomes.","A synthesis describes providers' preferred practices. What can a course responsibly infer?","Useful questions for service review, with actual survivor experience and effects still to assess.",["A proven Nigerian treatment effect.","A guarantee that every described practice is consistently implemented.","Permission to ignore affected people's feedback."],"Qualitative provider evidence has a different scope from demonstrated outcomes."),
("Closure targets should not determine a professional case decision.","A manager wants a case closed solely to meet a monthly count. What should the review consider?","The relevant professional process, current circumstances, preferences and continuing support needs.",["Only the target total.","A presumption that file closure proves safety.","Deletion of unresolved concerns from the record."],"Administrative counts do not establish an appropriate closure decision."),
("A fictional plan is distinct from credential authorisation.","A learner completes a fictional case-management outline. What does that alone authorise?","Neither a specialist caseworker designation nor a school certificate.",["Independent specialist practice without supervision.","A final ParaLearn school-exam pass.","Clinical or forensic registration."],"Learning evidence and professional or school credential requirements remain separate."),
]),
])
